<td id="S4.T5.4.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T5.4.3.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">51.27</span> / 6.2</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">TRAAC<span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> scales with longer response length.</span></span> During <span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TRAAC</span> training, we set a maximum token budget of 10k. To test the scalability of our method, we increase the max response length for both training and testing to 15k. <a href="#S4.T5" title="In 4.2 Ablations and Analysis ‣ 4 Result and Discussion ‣ Think Right:​ Learning to Mitigate Under-Over Thinking via Adaptive, Attentive Compression" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows the accuracy and average response length for AIME, AMC, and GPQA-D datasets, for the Qwen3-4B and <span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TRAAC</span> with increased token budget.
Similar to the prior results, we see an average accuracy improvement of 3.5% and 23.4% efficiency gains. This underscores that scaling <span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TRAAC</span> still shows consistent gains for both accuracy and efficiency.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T6" class="ltx_table ltx_align_floatright" style="width:58%;">
